(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-1-ans-cholinergic-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"ANS and Cholinergic Drugs Quiz 2 — Pharmacology I Exam 1","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What do ganglionic blockers do, and why are they rarely used?","choices":["They act at the neuromuscular junction to relax skeletal muscle","They block alpha and beta receptors simultaneously","They block nicotinic receptors at sympathetic and parasympathetic ganglia","They block muscarinic receptors only, sparing the sympathetic system"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and blocking everything at once is precisely why they are rarely used clinically. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Where are alpha-one receptors found, against alpha-two?","choices":["Alpha-one presynaptically and alpha-two postsynaptically","Both exclusively on the postsynaptic membrane","Both exclusively within the central nervous system","Alpha-one postsynaptically, alpha-two presynaptically"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and that presynaptic location is what makes an alpha-two agonist reduce sympathetic outflow. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"Which set matches the nicotinic effects?","choices":["Mydriasis, tachycardia, weakness, hypertension and fasciculations","Miosis, bradycardia, bronchospasm, lacrimation and salivation","Sedation, hyperthermia, flushing, dry mouth and urinary retention","Vasodilation, atony of the bladder, cycloplegia and xerostomia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and note this set moves the pupil and heart in the opposite direction to the muscarinic set. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which poisoning does physostigmine treat?","choices":["Overdose of a cholinesterase inhibitor insecticide","Beta blocker overdose","Opioid overdose","Overdose of anticholinergic drugs such as atropine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It raises acetylcholine to compete with the blocking drug. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which adverse effects do the older bladder agents cause?","choices":["Fasciculations and muscle weakness","Rebound hypertension and sedation","Dry mouth, constipation and blurred vision","Bradycardia, bronchospasm and diarrhoea"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, which are the predictable antimuscarinic effects. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which antimuscarinic is approved for axillary hyperhidrosis?","choices":["Scopolamine","Glycopyrronium","Dicyclomine","Tiotropium"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, approved in 2018 for that indication. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Where are M2 receptors found?","choices":["Neurons and gastric parietal cells","Neurons, bladder, exocrine glands and smooth muscle","Adrenal medulla and the neuromuscular junction","Neurons, cardiac cells and smooth muscle"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which is why muscarinic stimulation slows the heart. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What does pilocarpine do in the eye?","choices":["Has no ocular effect and is used only for dry mouth","Miosis and ciliary muscle contraction, lowering intraocular pressure","Causes mydriasis and cycloplegia, raising intraocular pressure","Reduces production of aqueous humour without changing pupil size"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with onset within minutes and a duration of several hours. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What is the rank order of potency at alpha receptors?","choices":["Epinephrine at least equal to norepinephrine, far above isoproterenol","Isoproterenol greater than epinephrine greater than norepinephrine","Norepinephrine greater than isoproterenol greater than epinephrine","All three are equipotent at alpha receptors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the beta order runs the other way with isoproterenol first. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Why is succinylcholine suited to rapid sequence intubation?","choices":["Its lack of any cardiovascular effect","Its reversibility with neostigmine","Its rapid onset and short duration of action","Its long duration, which covers the whole procedure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with the short duration owing to rapid breakdown by plasma enzyme. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"What is another term for an indirect-acting cholinergic agonist?","choices":["Parasympatholytic","Antimuscarinic","Choline ester","Anticholinesterase"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. They raise acetylcholine by blocking its degradation rather than binding the receptor. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"How does succinylcholine differ mechanistically from the nondepolarizing blockers?","choices":["It blocks calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum","It depolarizes the junction like acetylcholine but is not rapidly destroyed","It competitively prevents acetylcholine from binding without activating the receptor","It inhibits acetylcholinesterase in the junction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Continued binding leaves the receptor unable to transmit further impulses. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"How is the nicotinic receptor structured?","choices":["An enzyme-linked receptor that phosphorylates intracellular targets on binding","An intracellular receptor that binds acetylcholine after it crosses the membrane","Five subunits forming a ligand-gated ion channel opened by two acetylcholine molecules","A seven-transmembrane receptor coupled to a G protein and a second messenger"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it is found in the central nervous system, adrenal medulla, autonomic ganglia and neuromuscular junction. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"How are the neuromuscular blocking drugs described structurally?","choices":["As structural analogues of norepinephrine acting at adrenergic receptors","As enzymes that degrade acetylcholine at the endplate","As calcium channel blockers acting on the sarcoplasmic reticulum","Structural analogues of acetylcholine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and both are used during surgery to produce complete muscle relaxation. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Which muscarinic receptor subtypes are functionally characterised?","choices":["M2 and M4 only, the cardiac subtypes","M1, M2 and M3 only, of five subclasses","All five subclasses, M1 through M5","M1 and M2 only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. M4 and M5 are listed as neuronal without further characterisation. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What are the two limits on pralidoxime?","choices":["It does not enter the brain, and cannot overcome reversible inhibitors","It works only in children, and only within one hour of exposure","It must be given orally, and it is inactivated by food","It reverses muscarinic but not nicotinic signs"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It reactivates inhibited acetylcholinesterase but only within those limits. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"What are the two sections of an autonomic efferent pathway?","choices":["A preganglionic neuron centrally, then a postganglionic one","One myelinated neuron straight to the effector organ","Two preganglionic neurons in series, both within the central nervous system","A postganglionic neuron in the cord and a preganglionic neuron in the periphery"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the postganglionic neuron is generally nonmyelinated. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What must a patient do after handling a scopolamine patch?","choices":["Wash their hands thoroughly afterwards","Avoid showering for twenty-four hours so the patch adheres","Apply the patch to the same site each time to maintain levels","Take an antihistamine alongside it to prevent sedation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is a small instruction that prevents an alarming symptom. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which statement about antimuscarinic agents is correct?","choices":["They act mainly at the skeletal neuromuscular junction","They block sympathetic adrenergic receptors as well as muscarinic ones","They do not block nicotinic receptors","They block nicotinic receptors at ganglia as their principal action"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and they leave the actions of sympathetic stimulation unopposed. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Diarrhoea, urination, small pupils, slow heart, wet lungs, vomiting, tearing, drooling. Which picture?","choices":["Ganglionic blockade","Muscarinic stimulation","Nicotinic stimulation","Antimuscarinic toxicity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture groups these as defecation, urination, miosis, bradycardia, bronchorrhoea, bronchospasm, emesis, lacrimation and salivation. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which adverse effect is specific to atracurium?","choices":["Rebound tachycardia on withdrawal","Histamine release, with a fall in blood pressure and flushing","Malignant hyperthermia in susceptible people","Prolonged paralysis from pseudocholinesterase deficiency"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it was replaced by its isomer cisatracurium for having fewer adverse effects. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Into which two groups do the direct-acting cholinergic agonists divide?","choices":["Reversible and irreversible acetylcholinesterase inhibitors","Antimuscarinics and ganglionic blockers","Catecholamines and noncatecholamines","Choline esters and naturally occurring alkaloids"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The esters are synthetic apart from acetylcholine itself. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What does the afferent division do, and what is an example?","choices":["It connects preganglionic to postganglionic neurons within a ganglion","It controls gastrointestinal motility independently of the brain","It carries signals to the central nervous system","It carries signals away from the central nervous system to skeletal muscle"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The reflex arc needs the incoming limb before any autonomic outflow can be adjusted. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which adverse effect does physostigmine cause at high doses?","choices":["Malignant hyperthermia","Rebound hypertension on abrupt withdrawal","First-dose syncope","Convulsions, bradycardia and skeletal muscle paralysis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The paralysis comes from too much transmitter rather than too little. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What does nicotine do at the nicotinic receptor as concentration rises?","choices":["It stimulates the receptor at low concentrations and blocks it at high concentrations","It stimulates the receptor at all concentrations, increasing with dose","It blocks the receptor at all concentrations","It has no effect on the nicotinic receptor, acting only at muscarinic sites"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which is why nicotine appears in both the agonist and the ganglionic blocker discussions. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"How do the indirect-acting cholinergic agents work?","choices":["They block reuptake of acetylcholine into the presynaptic terminal","They increase the activity of acetylcholine by inhibiting acetylcholinesterase","They bind muscarinic receptors directly and mimic acetylcholine","They increase synthesis of acetylcholine from acetyl coenzyme A and choline"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. They raise the transmitter rather than binding the receptor themselves. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which muscles does a nondepolarizing blocker paralyse first?","choices":["All skeletal muscle simultaneously","The small muscles of the face and eye","The diaphragm and intercostal muscles","The muscles of the trunk and neck"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the diaphragm goes last, with recovery in reverse order. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"From what is acetylcholine synthesised?","choices":["Acetyl coenzyme A and choline","Tyrosine and dopamine","Glutamate and glycine","Tryptophan and serotonin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and choline is recovered by recycling after the transmitter is degraded. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What distinguishes scopolamine from atropine?","choices":["It stimulates muscarinic receptors rather than blocking them","It is a selective bladder agent","Greater central action, preventing motion sickness","It has no central effects and acts purely peripherally"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with sedation at lower doses and excitement at higher ones. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"How does atropine's cardiovascular effect vary with dose?","choices":["Bradycardia at lower doses and tachycardia at higher doses","Tachycardia at lower doses and bradycardia at higher doses","Bradycardia at every dose","No cardiac effect, since its action is confined to secretions"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it is given during a code to treat bradycardia. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 49"}]};
